"A Feature is a physical endurant which has a unity criterion and is existentially dependent on another physical endurant, its host (examples: a hole, a bump, an object’s boundary, a stain on a t-shirt)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Feature is a physical endurant which has a unity criterion and is existentially dependent on another physical endurant, its host (examples: a hole, a bump, an object’s boundary, a stain on a t-shirt)." .
"A Mental Object is a non-physical object which existentially depends on an agentive physical object (examples: a percept, a sense datum)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Mental Object is a non-physical object which existentially depends on an agentive physical object (examples: a percept, a sense datum)." .
"A Non-Agentive Physical Object is a physical object to which intentions, believes and desires are not ascribed (examples: a pebble, a house, a computer, a human body)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Non-Agentive Physical Object is a physical object to which intentions, believes and desires are not ascribed (examples: a pebble, a house, a computer, a human body)." .
"A Non-Agentive Social Object is a social object to which intentions, believes and desires are not ascribed (examples: a law, an economic system, a currency, an asset)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Non-Agentive Social Object is a social object to which intentions, believes and desires are not ascribed (examples: a law, an economic system, a currency, an asset)." .
"A Non-Physical Endurant is an endurant with no direct spatial quality (examples: democracy, the United Nations, the general secretary of Amnesty International)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Non-Physical Endurant is an endurant with no direct spatial quality (examples: democracy, the United Nations, the general secretary of Amnesty International)." .
"A Non-Physical Object is a non-physical endurant with unity criterion (examples: a theory, a topic, a concept)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Non-Physical Object is a non-physical endurant with unity criterion (examples: a theory, a topic, a concept)." .
"A Particular is an entity that cannot have instances, in this sense it is opposed to universals (examples: a person, a soccer game, a plan, the color red, a feeling, a fact, a theory, number forty-two)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Particular is an entity that cannot have instances, in this sense it is opposed to universals (examples: a person, a soccer game, a plan, the color red, a feeling, a fact, a theory, number forty-two)." .
"A Perdurant is an entity that happens in time (examples: a person’s life, a soccer game, the reading of a book, the state of feeling happy). Some perdurants are temporally atomic (example: the change of a letter’s state due to the breaking of the seal)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Perdurant is an entity that happens in time (examples: a person’s life, a soccer game, the reading of a book, the state of feeling happy). Some perdurants are temporally atomic (example: the change of a letter’s state due to the breaking of the seal)." .
"A Physical Endurant is an endurant with direct spatial quality (examples: a person, a tree, an atom, the water in a glass, a hole in a wall, the center of the Earth)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Physical Endurant is an endurant with direct spatial quality (examples: a person, a tree, an atom, the water in a glass, a hole in a wall, the center of the Earth)." .
"A Physical Object is a physical endurant with unity criterion (examples: a person, a human body, a house, a computer)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Physical Object is a physical endurant with unity criterion (examples: a person, a human body, a house, a computer)." .
"A Physical Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a physical endurant (examples: the weight of a pen, the color of an apple)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Physical Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a physical endurant (examples: the weight of a pen, the color of an apple)." .
"A Physical Region is a region in a quality space for physical qualities (examples: the physical space, an area in the color quality space, 80Kg)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Physical Region is a region in a quality space for physical qualities (examples: the physical space, an area in the color quality space, 80Kg)." .
"A Process is a stative perdurant such that at some temporal scale some parts are of a different type  (examples: running, writing)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Process is a stative perdurant such that at some temporal scale some parts are of a different type  (examples: running, writing)." .
"A Quality is an entity that inheres in an endurant or a perdurant, and that can be perceived or measured (examples: the shape of a book, the color of a car, the size of a t-shirt, the electrical charge of a battery)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Quality is an entity that inheres in an endurant or a perdurant, and that can be perceived or measured (examples: the shape of a book, the color of a car, the size of a t-shirt, the electrical charge of a battery)." .
"A Region is an abstract entity classified by a quality type and with mereological structure, the sum of all the regions of a quality type is called a quality space (examples: the color red is a region in the quality space of color, the red of a rose is a (sub)region of the red region in the quality space of color, the commercial value of 1 Euro is a region in the quality space of commercial values)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Region is an abstract entity classified by a quality type and with mereological structure, the sum of all the regions of a quality type is called a quality space (examples: the color red is a region in the quality space of color, the red of a rose is a (sub)region of the red region in the quality space of color, the commercial value of 1 Euro is a region in the quality space of commercial values)." .
"A Social Agent is an individual agentive social object (examples: a teacher, a president, a software agent)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Social Agent is an individual agentive social object (examples: a teacher, a president, a software agent)." .
"A Social Object is a non-physical object which is generically dependent on a community of agents (examples: a person in the legal sense, a client, a law, an economic system)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Social Object is a non-physical object which is generically dependent on a community of agents (examples: a person in the legal sense, a client, a law, an economic system)." .
"A Society is a collective agentive social object (examples: a nation, the FCA Group, Apple, the European Central Bank)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Society is a collective agentive social object (examples: a nation, the FCA Group, Apple, the European Central Bank)." .
"A Space Region is a physical region, possibly disconnected, for spatial locations (examples: the region occupied by Earth in this moment, the region where it snowed in the year 1900)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Space Region is a physical region, possibly disconnected, for spatial locations (examples: the region occupied by Earth in this moment, the region where it snowed in the year 1900)." .
"A Spatial Location is the individual spatial quality of a physical endurant (examples: the spatial quality of the Earth, the spatial quality of a hole, the spatial quality of Adolf Anderssen)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Spatial Location is the individual spatial quality of a physical endurant (examples: the spatial quality of the Earth, the spatial quality of a hole, the spatial quality of Adolf Anderssen)." .
"A State is a stative perdurant such that all its parts are of the same type (examples: being sitting, being open, being happy, being red)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A State is a stative perdurant such that all its parts are of the same type (examples: being sitting, being open, being happy, being red)." .
"A Stative is a cumulative perdurant, i.e., a perdurant that when summed to another perdurant of the same type, gives a perdurant of the same type (examples: sitting, walking, waiting)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Stative is a cumulative perdurant, i.e., a perdurant that when summed to another perdurant of the same type, gives a perdurant of the same type (examples: sitting, walking, waiting)." .
"A Temporal Location is a temporal quality of a perdurant and is specific to that perdurant, that is, two concurrent perdurants have distinct temporal locations. The temporal location is the temporal quality which has as quale the unique time interval at which the perdurant is exactly located (examples: the temporal location of World War I, the temporal location of the Anderssen-Kieseritzky chess play in 1851, the temporal location of the summers of the 20th century)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Temporal Location is a temporal quality of a perdurant and is specific to that perdurant, that is, two concurrent perdurants have distinct temporal locations. The temporal location is the temporal quality which has as quale the unique time interval at which the perdurant is exactly located (examples: the temporal location of World War I, the temporal location of the Anderssen-Kieseritzky chess play in 1851, the temporal location of the summers of the 20th century)." .
"A Temporal Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a perdurant (example: the duration of World War I, the starting time of the 2000 Olympics)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Temporal Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a perdurant (example: the duration of World War I, the starting time of the 2000 Olympics)." .
"A Temporal Region is a region in a quality space for temporal qualities (examples: a time interval, a historical period, a velocity)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Temporal Region is a region in a quality space for temporal qualities (examples: a time interval, a historical period, a velocity)." .
"A Time Interval is a temporal region which is an interval or a sum of intervals. A Time Interval can be atomic, i.e., instantaneous. (examples: the first second of your life,  the Cretaceous time period, the time interval of all winters of the third millennium)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A Time Interval is a temporal region which is an interval or a sum of intervals. A Time Interval can be atomic, i.e., instantaneous. (examples: the first second of your life,  the Cretaceous time period, the time interval of all winters of the third millennium)." .
"A constant atom is an endurant that has no constant proper parts" <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A constant atom is an endurant that has no constant proper parts" .
"A particular is either an abstract, an endurant, a perdurant, or a quality, and the classes Abstract, Endurant, Perdurant and Quality are all disjoint." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A particular is either an abstract, an endurant, a perdurant, or a quality, and the classes Abstract, Endurant, Perdurant and Quality are all disjoint." .
"A quality is either an abstract quality, a physical quality, or a temporal quality, and the classes AbstractQuality, PhysicalQuality, and TemporalQuality are all disjoint." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "A quality is either an abstract quality, a physical quality, or a temporal quality, and the classes AbstractQuality, PhysicalQuality, and TemporalQuality are all disjoint." .
