"An Abstract Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a non-physical endurant (examples: the value of an asset, the rights of the UN general secretary)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Abstract Quality is a quality that directly inheres to a non-physical endurant (examples: the value of an asset, the rights of the UN general secretary)." .
"An Abstract Region is a region in the abstract quality space for abstract qualities (example: the conventional value of 1 Euro)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Abstract Region is a region in the abstract quality space for abstract qualities (example: the conventional value of 1 Euro)." .
"An Abstract entity is an entity that has neither spatial nor temporal qualities and is not a quality itself (examples: a number, a set, a quality space)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Abstract entity is an entity that has neither spatial nor temporal qualities and is not a quality itself (examples: a number, a set, a quality space)." .
"An Accomplishment is an event which is mereologically non-atomic (examples: a conference, an ascent, a performance)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Accomplishment is an event which is mereologically non-atomic (examples: a conference, an ascent, a performance)." .
"An Achievement is an event which is mereologically atomic (examples: reaching the summit of K2, a departure, a death)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Achievement is an event which is mereologically atomic (examples: reaching the summit of K2, a departure, a death)." .
"An Agentive Physical Object is a physical object to which intentions, believes and desires are ascribed (examples: a human person as opposed to a legal person)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Agentive Physical Object is a physical object to which intentions, believes and desires are ascribed (examples: a human person as opposed to a legal person)." .
"An Agentive Social Object is a social object to which intentions, believes and desires are ascribed (examples: a teacher, a president, a software agent, a nation)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Agentive Social Object is a social object to which intentions, believes and desires are ascribed (examples: a teacher, a president, a software agent, a nation)." .
"An Amount Of Matter is a physical endurant which has no unity criterion and is mereologically invariant, that is, all its part are essential parts (examples: the gold of my wedding ring, the sand used to make this glass)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Amount Of Matter is a physical endurant which has no unity criterion and is mereologically invariant, that is, all its part are essential parts (examples: the gold of my wedding ring, the sand used to make this glass)." .
"An Arbitrary Sum is an endurant which is the mereological sum of at least a physical and a non-physical endurant." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Arbitrary Sum is an endurant which is the mereological sum of at least a physical and a non-physical endurant." .
"An Endurant is an entity wholly present, i.e., all its proper parts are present, at any time that it is present (examples: a person, a tree, an atom, an idea)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Endurant is an entity wholly present, i.e., all its proper parts are present, at any time that it is present (examples: a person, a tree, an atom, an idea)." .
"An Event is an anti-cumulative perdurant, i.e., a perdurant that when summed to another perdurant of the same type, gives a perdurant of a different type (examples: closing a door, reaching the top of a mountain, breaking a seal)." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An Event is an anti-cumulative perdurant, i.e., a perdurant that when summed to another perdurant of the same type, gives a perdurant of a different type (examples: closing a door, reaching the top of a mountain, breaking a seal)." .
"An endurant is either an arbitrary sum, a physical endurant or a non-physical endurant, and the classes ArbitrarySum, PhysicalEndurant and NonPhysicalEndurant are all disjoint." <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs> "An endurant is either an arbitrary sum, a physical endurant or a non-physical endurant, and the classes ArbitrarySum, PhysicalEndurant and NonPhysicalEndurant are all disjoint." .
